Here is the complete, step-by-step implementation guide to achieve your objective.
By creating a two-interface setup on the FortiGate VM, you isolate the LAN traffic from the WAN traffic inside KVM. The Ubuntu host will handle both networks seamlessly over your single physical ens160 interface, and you will completely avoid the routing loops you encountered earlier.

Step 1: Create the New WAN Network Configuration File

First, create an XML configuration file for the new, dedicated WAN virtual network on the Ubuntu host.
1. Open a new file in the terminal:bash
sudo nano /tmp/fortigate-wan.xml
Use code with caution.
2. Paste the following configuration block into the file:xml
<network>
  <name>fgt-wan-net</name>
  <forward mode='nat'/>
  <bridge name='virbr1' stp='on' delay='0'/>
  <ip address='192.168.150.1' netmask='255.255.255.0'>
    <dhcp>
      <range start='192.168.150.10' end='192.168.150.50'/>
    </dhcp>
  </ip>
</network>
Use code with caution.
3. Save and close the file (Ctrl+O, Enter, then Ctrl+X).

Step 2: Initialize and Start the WAN Network in KVM

Load this XML configuration into Libvirt so KVM can build the virbr1 virtual switch interface.
Run these commands in your Ubuntu terminal:
bash
sudo virsh net-define /tmp/fortigate-wan.xml
sudo virsh net-start fgt-wan-net
sudo virsh net-autostart fgt-wan-net
Use code with caution.

Step 3: Attach the New Network Interface to the FortiGate VM

Now, plug a virtual cable from this new virbr1 network directly into your FortiGate virtual machine.
Run this command (replace FortigateVM with your exact VM name if different):
bash
sudo virsh attach-interface --domain FortigateVM --type network --source fgt-wan-net --model virtio --config --live
Use code with caution.

Step 4: Reset and Clean the Routing Table Loop on Ubuntu

Since your new network (virbr1) automatically manages NAT and outbound routing, you must remove the conflicting policy rules and manual iptables entries that caused the previous routing loops.
Run these cleanup commands on the Ubuntu host terminal:
bash
# Remove the old interface-based policy routing rule
sudo ip rule del iif virbr0 table fgt_internet 2>/dev/null

# Clear out any custom forward rules we added manually to iptables
sudo iptables -F FORWARD
Use code with caution.

Step 5: Configure the Ubuntu Host Netplan Profile Permanently

Update your Netplan profile on the Ubuntu host so it cleanly holds the standard gateway configuration and maps the 1.1.1.1 target traffic straight down into the FortiGate's LAN port.
1. Open your configuration file:bash
sudo nano /etc/netplan/01-netcfg.yaml
Use code with caution.
2. Update the configuration to reflect a clean path:yaml
network:
  version: 2
  renderer: networkd
  ethernets:
    ens160:
      dhcp4: false
      addresses:
        - 192.168.1.100/24
      routes:
        # Default internet path for the Ubuntu host itself
        - to: default
          via: 192.168.1.1
        # Pure routing rule: Send the client's 1.1.1.1 traffic straight to FortiGate LAN
        - to: 1.1.1.1/32
          via: 192.168.122.71
Use code with caution.
3. Apply the updated settings:bash
sudo netplan apply
Use code with caution.

Step 6: Configure Interfaces and Firewall Rules inside FortiGate

Access your FortiGate CLI using sudo virsh console FortigateVM (or via its temporary web interface address at 192.168.122.71) to wire up the ports:
1. Configure Port 1 (LAN Link):
Ensure port1 stays statically bound to the virbr0 network:text
config system interface
    edit port1
        set mode static
        set ip 192.168.122.71 255.255.255.0
        set allowaccess ping https ssh
    next
end
Use code with caution.
2. Configure Port 2 (WAN Link):
Configure port2 (the new interface added in Step 3) to automatically fetch its IP and default internet gateway from Ubuntu's new virbr1 DHCP server:text
config system interface
    edit port2
        set mode dhcp
        set allowaccess ping
    next
end
Use code with caution.
3. Create the Outbound Firewall Policy:
Tell the FortiGate to allow traffic arriving from your physical client on port1 to exit towards the internet on port2 with NAT enabled:text
config firewall policy
    edit 1
        set name "LAN-to-Internet"
        set srcintf "port1"
        set dstintf "port2"
        set srcaddr "all"
        set dstaddr "all"
        set action accept
        set schedule "always"
        set service "ALL"
        set nat enable
    next
end
Use code with caution.

Step 7: Verify Everything works

Go back to your client machine (192.168.1.10) and start your test ping to 1.1.1.1.
Open your FortiGate sniffer console to observe the clean flow:
text
diagnose sniffer packet any "host 1.1.1.1" 4
Use code with caution.
What you will see now:
• Packet enters port1 safely: 192.168.1.10 -> 1.1.1.1 (Clean source IP preserved).
• Packet exits port2 safely: 192.168.150.x -> 1.1.1.1 (Handed over to Ubuntu for internet exit).
• No more loops or port unreachable errors!
